<%#
  views/reporter/dashboard.ejs

  The reporter's dashboard: a table of the logged-in reporter's own articles, newest
  update first, each with its status label and the actions allowed for that status.
  The whole table is rendered on the server, so no browser script is needed for it. The
  only script is public/js/reporter/dashboard.js, for the "New article" button and the
  "Submit for approval" buttons.

  Rendered by: controllers/reporterController.js (showReporterDashboard), for GET /reporter.
  Locals: title (the page title), articles (items from findReporterArticles: id, title,
  summary, category, status, statusLabel, isLive, editorNote, updatedAt),
  STATUS (the status names from services/articleWorkflow.js).
  Includes: the shared head, header and footer (views/partials).
  Owner: EZ.

  Why it is written this way (questions a lecturer may ask):
  - XSS: every piece of text a user wrote (title, category, the editor's note) is printed
    with the escaping output tag, the one with an equals sign. It turns < and > into
    harmless text, so a title like "<script>alert(1)</script>" is shown as text and
    never runs. The raw output tag (with a dash) does NOT escape; it is used here only
    to include our own partials (head, header, footer), which are trusted files and
    never user text.
  - Comments: these are EJS comments (the tag with a hash sign), not HTML comments. An
    HTML comment is sent to the browser and anyone can read it with "View page source";
    an EJS comment is removed on the server. Never write the closing percent-and-bracket
    characters inside one, because EJS would end the comment there.
  - Actions per status: Edit for draft, returned and published; "Submit for approval"
    only for draft and returned; no button for pending, because a pending article is
    waiting for the editor and the reporter must not change it (requirements: transitions
    are only allowed along the defined process). The server enforces this too (409 on a
    pending article, and canTransition on submit); hiding a button is never the real permission.
  - The "New article" button and the "Submit for approval" buttons are handled by
    public/js/reporter/dashboard.js. Edit leads to the editor page (articleEditor.ejs).
  - The editor's note is shown while an article is returned or pending again, so it is
    still readable after the reporter resubmits.
%>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<%# Trusted partial of ours (raw output is fine); the title is escaped inside head.ejs. %>
<%- include('../partials/head', { title: title }) %>

<body class="page-container">
  <%- include('../partials/header') %>

  <main class="main-content">
    <h2>My articles</h2>

    <%# The button is outside the if below, so a reporter with no articles can still create the first one.
        public/js/reporter/dashboard.js finds these two elements by id: it sends the request when the
        button is clicked and writes any error message into the empty paragraph (role="alert" makes
        screen readers announce it). %>
    <p>
      <button type="button" id="new-article-button" class="btn btn-primary">New article</button>
    </p>
    <p id="new-article-error" class="form-error" role="alert"></p>

    <%# A new reporter has no articles. Say so, instead of showing an empty table. %>
    <% if (articles.length === 0) { %>
      <p class="empty-state">You have no articles yet.</p>
    <% } else { %>
      <%# On a narrow screen the table scrolls sideways inside this box, not the whole page
          (table-scroll is the shared class in public/css/main.css, used by every table). %>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Title</th>
              <th scope="col">Category</th>
              <th scope="col">Status</th>
              <th scope="col">Last update</th>
              <th scope="col">Actions</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <%# One row per article. The controller already sorted them, newest update first. %>
            <% articles.forEach(function (article) { %>
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <%# An empty draft has no title yet, so show a placeholder instead of an empty cell. %>
                  <%# The title is typed by a user, so it is printed escaped (XSS protection). %>
                  <%= article.title || '(Untitled)' %>

                  <%# The editor's reason for returning the article, shown right next to its title.
                      It stays visible after the reporter resubmits (the article is "pending" again),
                      so the reporter can still read what was asked of them until the editor decides. %>
                  <%# The controller sends a note only for returned and pending articles; the status
                      check here is a second guard. The note is text an editor typed, so it is escaped too. %>
                  <% if ((article.status === STATUS.RETURNED || article.status === STATUS.PENDING) && article.editorNote) { %>
                    <p class="form-error"><strong>Editor's note:</strong> <%= article.editorNote %></p>
                  <% } %>
                </td>
                <td><%= article.category || '-' %></td>
                <td>
                  <%# status-badge plus status-draft / status-pending / ... gives each status its colour. %>
                  <span class="status-badge status-<%= article.status %>"><%= article.statusLabel %></span>

                  <%# Readers still see the approved version while a new one is being prepared. %>
                  <%# Example: a live article the reporter is editing has the status badge "In
                      preparation" AND this second badge. If the status is already "Published", the
                      first badge says it, so a second one would only repeat it. %>
                  <% if (article.isLive && article.status !== STATUS.PUBLISHED) { %>
                    <span class="status-badge status-published">Live version online</span>
                  <% } %>
                </td>
                <td>
                  <%# datetime holds the exact machine-readable moment; the text shows the same
                      moment in the format used elsewhere on the site. It is formatted on the
                      server, so it uses the server's time zone. %>
                  <time datetime="<%= new Date(article.updatedAt).toISOString() %>">
                    <%= new Date(article.updatedAt).toLocaleString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'short', year: 'numeric', hour: '2-digit', minute: '2-digit' }) %>
                  </time>
                </td>
                <td>
                  <%# A pending article is waiting for the editor, so the reporter can't change it. %>
                  <% if (article.status === STATUS.PENDING) { %>
                    Waiting for the editor
                  <% } else { %>
                    <%# Draft, returned and published articles can be edited. This link opens the editor
                        page (articleEditor.ejs). Editing a published article is allowed: its first
                        change moves the working copy back to "In preparation". %>
                    <a href="/reporter/articles/<%= article.id %>/edit" class="btn btn-primary">Edit</a>

                    <%# Submit only for "In preparation" and "Returned for corrections" (this includes a
                        live article whose new version is in preparation). An article that is plain
                        "Published" has nothing new to submit; the reporter edits it first, which moves
                        it back to "In preparation". The server checks all of this again, and also that a
                        live article really differs from its published version: if not it answers 409
                        "There are no changes to submit", which the script shows in this row.
                        public/js/reporter/dashboard.js handles the click for every such button, using
                        data-article-id, and writes errors into the paragraph with class "submit-error". %>
                    <% if (article.status === STATUS.DRAFT || article.status === STATUS.RETURNED) { %>
                      <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary submit-article-button" data-article-id="<%= article.id %>">Submit for approval</button>
                      <p class="form-error submit-error" role="alert"></p>
                    <% } %>
                  <% } %>
                </td>
              </tr>
            <% }); %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    <% } %>
  </main>

  <%- include('../partials/footer') %>

  <%# Order matters: public/js/reporter/dashboard.js calls apiClient, so apiClient.js must load first. %>
  <script src="/js/shared/apiClient.js"></script>
  <script src="/js/reporter/dashboard.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
